FINAL IVX AUTONOMOUS CODER IMPLEMENTATION
TURN IVX IA FROM A ROUTING SYSTEM INTO A REAL CODE-WRITING SENIOR DEVELOPER

CURRENT VERIFIED STATE

IVX IA currently can:

- classify developer requests;
- create persistent jobs;
- inspect files;
- run tests;
- report structured status;
- block fake execution claims;
- return task IDs;
- synchronize with the worker queue.

IVX IA currently cannot:

- independently decide what code must change;
- generate a patch;
- edit source files;
- validate its own patch;
- iterate after failed tests;
- commit its own verified change;
- deploy its own verified change.

Rork currently performs the actual coding.

OBJECTIVE

Implement a real owner-controlled autonomous coding loop inside IVX so IVX IA can:

Owner request
→ inspect repository
→ create technical plan
→ generate patch
→ modify files
→ run tests
→ analyze failures
→ revise patch
→ pass QA
→ create commit
→ request owner deployment approval
→ deploy
→ verify production
→ return evidence

Do not return another planning report.
Implement the actual coding loop.

==================================================
1. CREATE THE AUTONOMOUS CODING ENGINE
==================================================

Create a dedicated service such as:

ivx-autonomous-coder.ts

The service must accept:

{
  "taskId": "...",
  "goal": "...",
  "repository": "...",
  "branch": "...",
  "executionMode": "read_only | code_change | deploy",
  "ownerId": "...",
  "approvalPolicy": "owner_gated"
}

The service must run a bounded engineering loop:

INSPECT
→ PLAN
→ PATCH
→ TEST
→ ANALYZE
→ REVISE
→ VERIFY
→ COMMIT
→ AWAIT_OWNER_APPROVAL
→ DEPLOY
→ PRODUCTION_VERIFY

==================================================
2. REAL REPOSITORY WORKSPACE
==================================================

For every code-change job:

- create an isolated branch or worktree;
- fetch the latest owner-controlled repository;
- record starting SHA;
- verify clean working state;
- prevent unrelated files from entering the patch;
- lock the workspace to one task;
- persist checkpoints.

Do not edit production source directly without isolation.

==================================================
3. CODE INSPECTION
==================================================

The coder must be able to:

- search files;
- read source;
- inspect types;
- inspect routes;
- inspect tests;
- inspect logs;
- inspect API contracts;
- inspect database migrations;
- inspect related commits;
- identify the smallest safe change.

It must list all inspected files in the job ledger.

==================================================
4. PATCH GENERATION
==================================================

The coding model must generate an actual unified diff or structured file edits.

Each proposed edit must include:

- file path;
- reason;
- affected lines;
- expected behavior;
- risk;
- tests required.

The patch must be applied only inside the task workspace.

==================================================
5. TEST-AND-REPAIR LOOP
==================================================

After patching, automatically run:

- targeted unit tests;
- integration tests;
- typecheck;
- lint;
- build;
- relevant API tests;
- mobile smoke tests when applicable.

If a test fails:

1. capture exact failure;
2. identify whether the patch caused it;
3. revise the patch;
4. rerun the failed test;
5. rerun regression tests.

Use a bounded loop.

Example:

maximumIterations: 5

If still failing after the limit:

STATUS: BLOCKED
include exact failures and current diff.

Do not claim completion.

==================================================
6. TOOL PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Give the autonomous coder controlled access to:

- repository read/write;
- shell commands in isolated workspace;
- test runner;
- typecheck;
- lint;
- Git;
- approved database inspection;
- approved deployment APIs;
- logs;
- health checks.

Do not give unrestricted production deletion access.

==================================================
7. OWNER APPROVAL GATES
==================================================

The coder may autonomously:

- inspect;
- edit code;
- run tests;
- create a branch;
- create a commit;
- prepare a build.

Require owner approval before:

- pushing to protected production branch;
- deploying production;
- destructive database migration;
- deleting production data;
- DNS changes;
- payment configuration changes;
- credential rotation;
- rollback involving data risk.

==================================================
8. REAL MODEL PROVIDER
==================================================

Connect the coder to a real coding-capable LLM provider under the owner’s account.

The model connection must be owner-controlled and not Rork-controlled.

Document:

- provider;
- model;
- credential variable name;
- usage limits;
- cost limits;
- fallback model;
- timeout;
- retry policy.

Do not expose secret values.

==================================================
9. STRUCTURED CODER OUTPUT
==================================================

Every autonomous coding job must persist:

- taskId;
- starting SHA;
- files inspected;
- root cause;
- technical plan;
- generated diff;
- files changed;
- commands run;
- test results;
- typecheck;
- lint;
- build result;
- final SHA;
- commit SHA;
- deployment ID;
- production verification;
- iteration count;
- cost;
- duration.

==================================================
10. PREVENT FAKE CODING
==================================================

A job cannot be marked COMPLETED unless:

- a real patch exists, or the exact requested behavior was proven already correct;
- relevant tests ran;
- typecheck ran when applicable;
- evidence belongs to the same task;
- commit exists when code changed;
- deployment evidence exists when deployment was requested.

Generic `bun test` plus `/health` is not sufficient for a code-change request.

==================================================
11. FIRST CONTROLLED TEST
==================================================

Use this small safe task:

“Change one visible version label in the IVX owner dashboard from AUTONOMOUS-CODER-PILOT-1 to AUTONOMOUS-CODER-PILOT-2. Run targeted tests, typecheck, create a commit, but do not deploy.”

Required proof:

1. One task ID.
2. Isolated workspace created.
3. Correct file found.
4. Actual diff generated.
5. File changed.
6. Targeted tests pass.
7. Typecheck passes.
8. Commit created.
9. No production deployment.
10. Job result comes from IVX IA, not Rork manually editing the code.

==================================================
12. SECOND TEST — REAL BUG FIX
==================================================

After the pilot passes, run:

“Reproduce and fix the chat ordering bug so the newest conversation opens first.”

Required proof:

- root cause;
- files inspected;
- patch;
- targeted regression test;
- test failure before fix;
- test pass after fix;
- typecheck;
- commit;
- owner approval;
- deployment;
- Android verification;
- production evidence.

==================================================
13. THIRD TEST — SELF-REPAIR
==================================================

Intentionally provide a patch with a failing test in a controlled branch.

Verify IVX IA:

- detects the failure;
- analyzes the error;
- revises its patch;
- reruns tests;
- succeeds or blocks honestly.

This proves it can iterate rather than only generate one patch.

==================================================
14. REMOVE RORK FROM THE CODING PATH
==================================================

After IVX autonomous coding is proven:

- IVX jobs must not depend on Rork’s browser or sandbox;
- IVX must use owner-controlled model credentials;
- IVX must use owner-controlled GitHub;
- IVX must use owner-controlled worker runtime;
- IVX must use owner-controlled deployment services;
- IVX must operate from the IVX app and dashboard.

Rork may assist with this implementation now, but after completion Rork must not be required for normal coding.

==================================================
15. REQUIRED FINAL EVIDENCE
==================================================

Return:

AUTONOMOUS CODER:
IMPLEMENTED / FAILED

MODEL PROVIDER:
Owner-controlled: YES / NO

WORKER RUNTIME:
Owner-controlled: YES / NO

FIRST PILOT:
Task ID:
Starting SHA:
Files changed:
Tests:
Typecheck:
Commit SHA:
Deployment: NOT REQUESTED

REAL BUG FIX:
Task ID:
Root cause:
Patch:
Tests:
Commit:
Deployment:
Production verification:

SELF-REPAIR TEST:
Initial failure:
Iteration count:
Final result:

RORK REQUIRED FOR NORMAL CODING:
YES / NO

FINAL STATUS:
IVX IS A REAL AUTONOMOUS CODER
or
PARTIAL — LIST EXACT MISSING CAPABILITY

Do not call IVX a senior developer until it can independently inspect, edit, test, revise, commit, and verify real code changes without Rork authoring the patch.
